<h2>Choosing seats when you buy Broadway tickets</h2>
<p>Theater seating is split into a few general zones. Knowing the differences helps you decide where your money goes furthest.</p>
<h3>Orchestra</h3>
<p>The orchestra is the main floor closest to the stage. Front rows put you near the performers, but sitting too close can mean looking up and missing some of the stage pictures. If you buy Broadway tickets in the orchestra, rows a bit further back, near the center, often give a better overall view.</p>
<h3>Mezzanine and balcony</h3>
<p>Raised levels give a wider look at choreography and stage design, which is especially nice for large musicals. Front mezzanine seats are often a favorite. Rear balcony seats cost less but sit further from the stage.</p>
<h3>Boxes and side sections</h3>
<p>Boxes and side seats can feel exclusive, but some have partial or angled views. Read each listing for notes and check the seating chart before you buy.</p>

<h2>What affects the price when you buy Broadway tickets</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The show's popularity.</strong> New hits, award-season favorites and shows with a well-known cast draw higher prices.</li>
  <li><strong>The performance.</strong> Evening and weekend performances are generally busier than matinees or weeknights.</li>
  <li><strong>Seat location.</strong> Center orchestra and front mezzanine are usually priced above sides and the rear balcony.</li>
  <li><strong>Time of year.</strong> Holidays and school breaks bring more visitors and more demand.</li>
  <li><strong>Run length.</strong> Limited engagements and closing weeks tend to fill up quickly.</li>
</ul>

<div class="so-callout">
  <p><strong>Good to know:</strong> A seat in the first rows of the mezzanine can offer a clearer view than a seat at the edge of the front orchestra, and it may cost less. Compare both when you buy Broadway tickets.</p>
</div>

<h2>When to buy Broadway tickets</h2>
<p>For a popular show on a weekend or holiday, buy Broadway tickets as early as you can so you have more seats to choose from, especially if you need several together. For performances that are not selling as fast, prices can move as the date approaches, but waiting does not always pay off. Watch for <a href="/ticket-deals">ticket deals</a> and check the <a href="/tickets-promo-code">promo code page</a> before you pay.</p>
<p>A <a href="https://www.broadwayleague.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">trade association for Broadway theaters and producers</a> publishes information on current productions and touring shows, which can help you decide what to see.</p>

<h2>Venue types to know before you buy Broadway tickets</h2>
<p>Broadway theaters are large, historic houses in New York's theater district. <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Off-Broadway" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Off-Broadway</a> theaters are smaller and often more intimate, and they stage new plays and revivals. Touring musicals play in performing arts centers and civic theaters across the country, and Las Vegas shows are performed in resort theaters that are built for large productions.</p>
<p>Each type has its own feel. A small off-Broadway house may have few seats and a short distance to the stage, while a large touring venue can have thousands of seats with several levels.</p>

